Ganju strides across the floor, pulls his goggles down around his neck, and grabs Ichigo’s face in one hand, fingers digging into his cheeks. “I asked you a question! What’s a Shinigami doing in west Rukongai?!”

Ichigo’s eye twitches. His fist comes up and lands square on Ganju’s jaw, and Ganju sails across the room and slides to a stop against the far wall.

Chad’s eyebrows climb. Orihime squeaks.

“What...” Ganju sits up, rubbing his chin. “You looking for a fight?”

“You’re the one who picked it!” Ichigo snaps. “You grabbed my face and started demanding to know who I am for no reason!”

“Please!” Orihime waves both hands. “Let’s not fight!”

The elder sighs into his sleeve. “I knew it would come to this.”

“Who is he?” Uryū asks.

Ganju gets to his feet, looking genuinely offended. “You don’t know who I am?” He thumps his chest. “I’m the deep-red bullet of west Rukongai! The boss of west Rukongai, acknowledged by all!”

“Self-proclaimed,” the elder says. “All of it. And he’s also the number-one Shinigami hater of west Rukongai. Self-proclaimed too.”

Ichigo and the others stare at him. Ganju doesn’t even blink. He lunges, wraps both arms around Ichigo’s middle, and drives him through the doorway. They crash into the dirt outside and roll.

I get up from my place by the wall and drift to the broken doorway to watch.

Uryū and Orihime hurry out after them and stop short. Dark shapes are filing out of the night on both sides, boars, a half dozen of them, each with a rider crouched low on its back. The lead boy kicks off and lands in front of them, arms crossed.

“Don’t mess with our boss,” Taichi Miyamoto says.

“If you try to help the Shinigami,” a second rider adds, “you’ll have to deal with the gang.”

Orihime’s mouth falls open. “They’re all riding boars...”

Ganju shoves Ichigo off him and jabs a finger at his face. “Get out! No Shinigami will ever set foot in west Rukongai while I’m around!”

Ichigo plants a boot in his face and pushes him backward. “Hear me out first, then we can finish this.”

Ganju stumbles, one hand clapped to his nose. “You hit me in the face twice!”

“Ichigo.” Yoruichi’s voice carries from the doorway. “Stop wasting energy on a pointless fight.”

“He started it!”

Yoruichi’s tail lashes once, hard enough to flick dust off the floor.

“If you won’t leave on your own, we’ll do this for real.” Ganju yanks a short sword from his belt. The elder wrings his hands. “Ganju, he’s a good Shinigami. He...”

“There’s no such thing! All Shinigami are equally bad!” Ganju charges. Uryū’s head snaps toward Ichigo’s empty hands. “Kurosaki! Your sword!” Orihime raises her fingers to her hairpins, but Taichi glares at her, and before she can say the words, Chad hauls a long wrapped bundle from the house and hurls it across the yard.

“Ichigo!”

Ichigo catches it, tears off the cloth, and plants himself as the blade flashes toward his ribs. Ganju takes a look at the huge sword. “That’s a big one.”

“Yeah, you want to see how big?”

Ganju’s horizontal slash comes in. Ichigo drives Zangetsu point-down into the dirt and blocks it flat against the blade. “Size isn’t everything,” Ganju growls. He slides one foot over the ground beside the sword, and the dirt under it seems to run like water. The ground turns to sand, and Zangetsu sinks up to its hilt with a hiss.

“What?!” Ichigo yanks at the handle. It won’t budge.

“He turned the ground into sand,” Uryū murmurs.

Ganju’s heel catches Ichigo’s temple. He bounces across the yard and rolls onto his feet. “Strange technique,” he mutters. When Ganju lunges again, Ichigo blocks the kick with one arm, grabs his pant leg with the other, and hauls him down. His fist crunches into Ganju’s face. “That sting? I could even beat Tatsuki at karate until junior high.”

Ganju snarls and swings again. Ichigo catches his wrist, drags him forward, and drives a second punch into his nose. A line of blood runs down Ganju’s lip, but he stays on his feet.

I lean against the doorframe and watch.

“You’re tough.” Ichigo pants. “I hit you with everything I had...”

A shrill alarm rings out. Taichi jumps. A little clock strapped to his back is going off.

“Boss!” Ishino yelps, checking it. “It’s nine o’clock!”

Ganju’s blood-streaked face goes slack. “What?! Already?!” He whistles. “Bonnie!”

The big boar in the doorway pounds past me, close enough that I feel the wind of her, leaps clean over Ichigo’s stunned head, and bowls Ganju over. He grabs a fistful of her bristles. “Bonnie, quit playing games! We don’t have time for this tonight.” He wipes his nose. “Let me ride you.”

“You’re running away?” Ichigo says.

“I’m not! Wait here until I get back.” Ganju swings onto Bonnie’s back and grins at him over his shoulder. “Don’t turn to fluff in the wind, dandelion!”

“Who’re you calling a dandelion?!”

The gang whoops, wheels their boars around, and thunders off into the dark, arguing loudly about setting the timer earlier. Ichigo is left standing in the yard with his empty hands, glowering after them. Uryū pats his shoulder.

Everything goes quiet. Dust drifts across the churned-up dirt.
